the target language is: prolog.

% A query that is non-terminating in one scenario and terminating in another,
% used to exercise query interruption in the editor.
%
% In scenario 'base' the first condition holds, so the body reaches the classic
% infinite loop 'repeat, fail' (via the Prolog escape) and the query never
% returns — it must be interrupted by the user. In scenario 'notLooping' the
% first condition fails, so the query terminates (with no answers).
%
% NOTE: there is intentionally no 'expects answers' for query 'loop', so the
% automated test suite never actually runs the looping case.
%
% (Avoid the word 'scenario' inside a template — it is a reserved section
% keyword, so a predicate like "the scenario is loaded" would not parse.)

the predicates are:
    it loops forever.
    the setup is complete.
    the setup is pending.

the knowledge base loop includes:
    it loops forever if
        the setup is complete and
        prolog (repeat, fail).

scenario base is:
    the setup is complete.

scenario notLooping is:
    the setup is pending.

query loop is:
    it loops forever.
